🏢 On-Premises vs Hosted vs Cloud

# 1. Azure storage service

Azure Storage Service is a cloud storage solution from Microsoft Azure that lets you store and manage data in different formats—scalable, secure, and highly available.

🔹 Main Types of Azure Storage
>Blob Storage (Object storage)
Stores unstructured data like images, videos, backups, logs
Common use: media files, data lakes
Example: storing user-uploaded photos in an app

>File Storage (Azure Files)
Managed file shares accessible via SMB/NFS
Works like a network drive in the cloud
Example: shared folders for applications or teams

>Queue Storage
Stores messages for communication between services
Useful for decoupling components in distributed systems
Example: background job processing

>Table Storage (NoSQL key-value store)
Stores structured data with flexible schema
Fast and cost-effective for large datasets
Example: user profiles, metadata

>Disk Storage
Managed disks for Azure Virtual Machines
Example: OS disks, data disks for VMs

🔹 Key Features
Scalability – Automatically scales to handle massive data
High Availability – Replication options (LRS, GRS, ZRS)
Security – Encryption at rest & in transit, RBAC, SAS tokens
Cost-effective – Pay-as-you-go pricing
Integration – Works with Azure services like VMs, Functions, Databricks

🔹 Storage Account
All storage services are grouped under a Storage Account, which acts as a container for your data.

🔹 Simple Architecture Example
App → Azure Blob Storage → Data stored → Access via API/SDK


# 2. OS Virtualization (Operating System Virtualization)
🔹 What is OS Virtualization?

OS Virtualization is a technique where multiple isolated environments (virtual systems) run on a single physical machine using the same operating system kernel.

👉 In simple words:
1 computer → multiple virtual environments → each behaves like its own system

🔹 How it Works
There are two main approaches:

1. Hypervisor-based Virtualization (VMs)
A hypervisor creates full virtual machines
Each VM has its own OS
Examples:
VMware
VirtualBox
Microsoft Hyper-V

👉 Structure:
Hardware → Hypervisor → Multiple VMs → Each VM has OS + Apps

2. OS-level Virtualization (Containers)
Uses the same OS kernel
Lightweight and fast
Examples:
Docker
Kubernetes

👉 Structure:
Hardware → Host OS → Containers → Apps only (no separate OS)

# Type 1 vs Type 2 Virtualization

# a. Virtualization
What is it:
Running multiple virtual machines (VMs) on one physical system using a hypervisor.

Key Points:
Each app runs inside a separate OS (Guest OS)
Heavy → needs more memory & resources
More isolation (secure but slower)

👉 Example: Running Windows, Linux, Mac all on one server


# b. Containerization
What is it:
Running multiple apps in containers using a container engine (like Docker), sharing the same OS.

Key Points:
No separate OS → uses Host OS
Lightweight & fast
Easy to scale and deploy

👉 Example: Running multiple apps using Docker containers

⚡ Final Difference (Simple):
Virtualization = Full OS per app (Heavy)
Containerization = Shared OS (Lightweight & Fast)